{"format_version":1,"partition":23499,"records":{"angewöhnten":{"entries":[["angewöhnten",null,null,null]],"senses":[[663985,"angewöhnten","verb","inflection of angewöhnen:",0],[663986,"angewöhnten","verb","first/third-person plural dependent preterite",1],[663987,"angewöhnten","verb","first/third-person plural dependent subjunctive II",2]],"inflections":[["angewöhnten","angewöhnen","first-person,indicative,plural,preterite,subordinate-clause"]]},"eltern":{"entries":[["eltern",null,"/ˈɛltərn/","From Middle High German eltern, from Old High German eltirōn, from Proto-Germanic *alþizô. Compare Dutch ouders, English elders.\nThe form is equivalent to a nominalised comparative of alt (“old”). The according spelling Ältern was common in Early Modern German, but later went out of fashion. Several sources claim that this was due to incomprehension of the etymology, but that seems implausible given its obviousness. An alternative explanation would be the desire to distinguish between Eltern (“parents”) and die Älteren (“older ones, elders”), which latter was commonly spelt Ältern.",{"g":"p","decl":"-|-|-|-||||"}]],"senses":[[15499,"eltern","noun","parents",0,"p"]],"inflections":[["eltern","elter","plural"]]},"erträglichen":{"entries":[["erträglichen",null,null,null]],"senses":[[334791,"erträglichen","adj","inflection of erträglich:",0],[334792,"erträglichen","adj","strong genitive masculine/neuter singular",1],[334793,"erträglichen","adj","weak/mixed genitive/dative all-gender singular",2],[334794,"erträglichen","adj","strong/weak/mixed accusative masculine singular",3],[334795,"erträglichen","adj","strong dative plural",4],[334796,"erträglichen","adj","weak/mixed all-case plural",5]],"inflections":[["erträglichen","erträglich","genitive,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"erz-":{"entries":[["erz-",null,"/ɛrts/","From Middle High German erz-, erze-, from Old High German erzi-, from Late Latin archi-, from Ancient Greek ἀρχι- (arkhi-)."]],"senses":[[45268,"erz-","